desktop-shell-system-status-line(1)

# NAME

desktop-shell-system-status-line - status line for the sway bar

# SYNOPSIS

*desktop-shell-system-status-line* [*-c* _FILE_]

# DESCRIPTION

*desktop-shell-system-status-line* is a status command for *swaybar*(5). It
writes the status line protocol (*swaybar-protocol*(7)) to standard output
and reads the bar's click events from standard input. It ends when the bar
closes either.

Blocks: windows asking for attention (from the compositor), network and
internet reachability (NetworkManager), traffic of the
main connection, Bluetooth (BlueZ), processor use, processor temperature,
memory, disk space, volume (PulseAudio protocol, as served by PipeWire),
brightness, power profile (power-profiles-daemon), battery (UPower),
keyboard layout (from the compositor through _$SWAYSOCK_), clock and the
latest notification of *desktop-shell-notification-daemon*(1), on the
session bus. A block whose source is missing is hidden.

A new notification is shown on the accent background for a few seconds,
then in the unread color until it is read; critical ones use the bar's
urgent colors at first. There are no popups.

Services report their changes; processor, temperature, memory, disk,
traffic and brightness are read on a timer at each block's interval. A line
is only written when it differs from the last one, and the clock wakes only
when the shown time changes.

# OPTIONS

*-h, --help*
	Show the help message and exit.

*-c, --config* _FILE_
	Read settings from _FILE_ instead of the usual places.

*-s, --style* _FILE_
	Accepted like by every component; unused, as the bar draws the text.

# CONFIGURATION

In the *bar* block of the sway configuration:

```
status_command desktop-shell-system-status-line
```

# SETTINGS

Read from _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/desktop-shell-components/system-status-line.ini_,
else _@sysconfdir@/xdg/desktop-shell-components/system-status-line.ini_, which
lists every setting with its default. All are optional; *swaymsg reload*
restarts the status command and applies changes.

*[status]*
	*order* = block names, left to right++
	*text-color*, *warning-color*, *accent-color*, *accent-text-color*,
	*unread-color*, *critical-color*, *quiet-color* = _#rrggbb_++
	*separator-width* = _0-100_++
	*pad-numbers* = _true_|_false_

Each block has a group named after it (*attention*, *notifications*, *network*, *traffic*, *bluetooth*,
*processor*, *temperature*, *memory*, *disk*, *volume*, *brightness*,
*power-profile*, *battery*, *keyboard-layout*, *clock*) with:

	*format*, *format-*_STATE_ = text with *{placeholders}* and Pango markup++
	*warning*, *urgent* = thresholds; _-1_ turns one off++
	*interval* = seconds, for blocks read on a timer++
	*left-click*, *middle-click*, *right-click*, *scroll-up*, *scroll-down* =
	a built-in action (*toggle-mute*, *volume-up*, *volume-down*,
	*brightness-up*, *brightness-down*, *next-power-profile*,
	*toggle-bluetooth*, *toggle-format*, *activate-notification*,
	*mark-notifications-read*, *toggle-do-not-disturb*,
	*focus-urgent-window*), a command run without a shell, or nothing

An empty format hides the block in that state.

Also *[notifications] width*, *new-notification-emphasis-seconds* and
*show-when-empty*, *[disk] path*, *[temperature] sensor* (a hwmon driver name),
*[volume] step* and *[brightness] step*. The clock's formats are
strftime-style, see *g_date_time_format*(3).

# SEE ALSO

*sway-bar*(5), *swaybar-protocol*(7), *desktop-shell-workspace-window-bar*(1)
